The native key behind each key event (Task 14 of docs/plans/20261014-gpui-frontend.md): GPUI's `Keystroke` keeps
neither a key's position nor its layout's unshifted character and drops Shift from symbols, which a terminal needs for
physical-key bindings and the Kitty keyboard protocol. The Wayland and X11 clients install a `NativeInput` for exactly
the dispatch of one key event (an RAII scope): a press, each of its Wayland autorepeats (with the press's own record)
and its release carry `NativeInput::Key` (XKB keycode, modifiers with Shift, consumed modifiers, the layout's level-0
character and whether that layout types ASCII, the text before Ctrl transformations, composition and repeat flags, the
keystroke GPUI built); text-input-v3's one-character commit, dispatched as a synthetic key, carries
`NativeInput::Text`. `current_native_input()` reads it during the dispatch. Linux only and additive: no GPUI type
changes, so gpui-kit, gpui-component and the other platforms build unchanged. scripts/vendor-gpui.sh applies this to
the verified crates.io archive.

